            "SRC-016"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "CLAR",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper is well-organized with a clear logical flow, progressing from the two-level framework definition to fast rates for participating and unparticipating clients, and finally to unbounded losses, with an introduction that effectively motivates the problem.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-004",
            "SRC-009"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "There is a notation inconsistency in the definition of the noise term $\\xi_i^j$; it is defined as the residual of the semi-empirical minimizer $\\widehat{h}^*$ in Equation (2) but redefined as the residual of the population minimizer $h^*$ in Definition 4, which is confusing and potentially incorrect.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-017",
            "SRC-018"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Clarify the definition of $\\xi_i^j$ by unifying the notation or clearly distinguishing the contexts, ensuring the noise term aligns with the specific minimizer relevant to the local Rademacher complexity bound."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The notation for Rademacher complexity terms is inconsistent in Section 4.2, where the text describes $\\omega_{mn}(\\mathcal{H} - h^*, \\eta)$ immediately after defining $\\omega_m(\\mathcal{H} - h^*, \\eta)$, using the wrong subscript.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-020"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Correct the text in Section 4.2 to refer to $\\omega_m(\\mathcal{H} - h^*, \\eta)$ instead of $\\omega_{mn}(\\mathcal{H} - h^*, \\eta)$ to match the definition provided in Definition 5."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "Figure 1 is referenced to illustrate the participation gap and error, but its caption is minimal and does not clearly describe the visual elements, forcing readers to rely heavily on the surrounding text to understand the two-level framework representation.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Expand the caption of Figure 1 to explicitly describe the visual elements, such as the meta-distribution at the top level, sampled client distributions at the bottom, and the representation of the participation gap."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper uses the terms \"participating gap\" and \"participation gap\" interchangeably, which reduces clarity despite likely referring to the same concept.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002",
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Choose one term, such as \"participation gap,\" and use it consistently throughout the paper."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "SIGN",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The work addresses a broadly important problem in federated learning by providing the first theoretical guarantees for unparticipating clients, which is critical for real-world FL systems where client participation is stochastic and incomplete.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-002",
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper improves upon the state-of-the-art by deriving fast learning rates in probability form, whereas previous works only provided bounds in expectation form, and by extending the analysis to unbounded losses which prior FL generalization bounds typically did not handle.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-003",
            "SRC-021"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The theoretical framework is highly relevant to the domain of federated learning, specifically addressing the practical challenges of client heterogeneity and non-participation that are central to cross-device FL deployment.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004",
            "SRC-005"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
